when covet 19 first emerged scientists  were scrambling to understand  the deadly disease  searching far and wide for answers  in a distant digital galaxy an unlikely  ally heard the call  an army of gamers were booted up  and ready to play a role in this  pandemic  i have the biggest lab in the world i  have more people working for me as a  scientist  than i think anybody else  our search to find out how gamers are  helping scientists understand kovid led  us to reykjavik iceland  where we found bc native david ecker  he's a production director for ccp games  the makers of the wildly popular online  title  eve  eve online is basically a spaceship  driven mmorpg  so massively multiplayer online game  there's a lot of choice in the roles  that people can play there is a you know  big open market big economy big  community within the game  there can be as many as 250 000 people  around the world playing at the same  time  an armchair army with massive processing  power at their fingertips  kovid's ccp knew it could harness that  power to do more than entertain its  players video games can just be such a  strong conduit for citizen science  to make that happen ecker was put in  touch with another canadian gamer  ryan brinkman who also happens to be a  research scientist at the bc cancer  research center ryan's like the coolest  scientist i think i've ever met or  envisioned he's not just a lab coat kind  of guy on this  [Music]  ryan's day job is building software  tools to help other scientists with  something called flow cytometry close to  data is a technology we use to look at  cells in the blood we use it for  infection for immunity  to understand and develop treatments for  things like cancer covin-19  the plan take information about how  covet infection changes human cells and  turn it into a game where players can  visually identify those altered cells  using real human samples  i didn't have a single data set when we  started i didn't know  where the first data set was going to  come from  those first samples were taken from  hospitals in northern italy where  medical staff were forced to make the  impossible choice  of which patients would live or die  to have it come in right from those  front lines was a bit of a surreal thing  if you really thought about it enough  but it also was you know a motivator for  us to really push this thing out get it  to the users let's really start learning  something  [Music]  the challenge explaining complicated  science in a quick easy and entertaining  way to the gamers that will ensure  valuable scientific results how does  this process work with  covet 19 looking at what the immune  system is responding to or not  responding to when that virus occurs but  we're looking for an increase in the  amount of certain cells or a decrease in  the amount of certain cells  usually a highly trained scientist  manually looks at the cells identifying  the differences between healthy and  unhealthy ones a massively time  consuming task  ryan realized a computer could do it  faster but that requires enough data to  build the right program  that's where the gamers come in they are  providing the information which will  teach the computer what cell patterns to  look for he was able to explain it to us  and then we're able to in turn take that  understanding and explain it to the  thousands of people that are playing our  game  that was kind of a make or break with  this entire thing  the mini game within the game is called  project discovery flow cytometer  in victoria michael daw has been an avid  eve explorer for more than a decade as  an ambassador for the game he wanted to  join the covet fight  and at the same time he was in the  middle of his own battle with cancer  when project discovery came out for the  covid research i was just about to start  chemotherapy so this was near and dear  to my heart it was  something that i felt like i was getting  involved with the science that was  affecting the whole planet  in the flow cytometer game players like  michael are tasked with lassoing a group  of dots they believe are the same type  of cell  i can definitely see that there is a  cluster here so i'm doing my best to  outline it  but i also see a whole bunch of little  dots down here so i'm going to call that  a second cluster and do my best outline  that  we had no idea the power of citizen  science we could be brought to this  problem where people really want to help  and are engaged  gamers have looked at more than 13  million samples the equivalent of over  510 years of work  so that is time that a group of  scientists would actually be having to  crunch the numbers on this  we've taken the data that the gamers  have given us and developed some baby  machine learning algorithms and we've  seen the performance is really  outstripping the current state of the  art which is incredible and we know it's  only get better with the data gets  better it's going to help not only  diagnosis because we don't have to have  really important people drawing circles  around dots explosive is also important  developing treatments treatments not  just for kovid but in the future  advancing research for other illnesses  like cancer i have a lot of pride in eve  online in the fact that we are one of  the pioneers in this that we're trying  to be something more than just a  distraction from reality that we are  helping the real world  by being gamers  at a time when so many are desperately  searching for an escape from this  pandemic  comes a game that proves you can be lost  along the outer edges of a distant  galaxy and still be proudly grounded in  helping humanity  we're making an impact and we're  reaching people  and  it just shows that people care  you
 

